      <h2 id="talc-h">$10 million verdict against Johnson &amp; Johnson baby powder</h2>
      <p>In <em>Roy v. Johnson &amp; Johnson</em>, Landry &amp; Swarr partner Matthew C. Clark helped represent the family of Lynne Roy, a New Orleans-area woman who died from mesothelioma in 2020. After a three-week trial, the jury found that Ms. Roy had significant exposure to asbestos in a Johnson &amp; Johnson product and that the exposure was a cause of her mesothelioma.</p>
      <p>Not every mesothelioma case starts at a shipyard. If you or a loved one used talc-containing products for years, the source of exposure may be in the bathroom cabinet.</p>
